A 28-year-old chef has been found dead in a shopping mall in San Francisco
A 28-year-old chef who recently moved to San Francisco was found dead this week in a stairwell at a local mall.
But the victim’s brother said he couldn’t think of anyone who would want to harm him, adding that “everything is finally getting better.”
San Francisco's coroner's office said the body found Wednesday morning's Nishida shopping center was identified as 28-year-old San Francisco resident Frank Galicia.
The San Francisco Police Department said the death was determined to kill him and an investigation is ongoing.
The victim’s older brother, Louis Galicia, told KGO, a San Francisco-based radio station that Frank, who previously worked as an assembly line chef in Boston six months ago, found an ideal job as an assembly line chef at the Sons & Daughters restaurant in San Francisco.
A spokesman for Sons & Daughters said they were “extremely shocked” by Frank’s death.
“We are a small team of families who are close to each other, and we will miss him dearly,” the spokesperson said.
We extend our deepest sympathies and condolences to Frank’s family and friends.
Louis Galicia said that Frank first lived in the guest house, but recently "everything is finally getting better."
Louis Galicia told KGO, “Frank found an apartment and socialized with a girl at the same time.”
Luis Galicia said he couldn’t figure out who would want to kill his younger brother.
Frank had a good heart and a broad heart.
Louis Galicia said, “Frank often cooks dinners for his family.”
He never wanted to argue with his family.
He’s a person with everyone’s opinions.
Louis Galicia once said, “Everything in the world can go wrong, but Frank can be called a coarse insider who shines every day.”
If you have a clue, please call the San Francisco Police Department on 415-575-4444.
Junior doctors on strike call for new labour action
The junior doctor’s representative called on the coalition to approve new labor actions in the context of new contract disputes.
The British Medical Association’s Board of Primary Physicians will require all of its members to support more labour action since the beginning of September.
The junior doctors’ committee said the ministers did not resolve their concerns about the contract.
In July, junior doctors and medical scientists voted to boycott a contract deal with the British Medical Association.
Fifty-eight per cent of the voting members opposed the contract transaction.
In a letter to its members published on Twitter on Thursday night, Ellen McCourt, chair of the Junior Doctors Committee, said the government was “consistently silent” on the issues leading to a boycott of contracts.
“In the light of this,” she said, “the Executive Committee of the Primary Physicians Committee has voted unanimously to reject the proposed new contract and to call for renewed formal consultations on all concerns,” she said.
In view of the government’s silence, the Executive Committee of the Primary Physicians Committee has today formally asked the Council of the British Medical Association to hold a special meeting to approve a long-term plan to escalate labour action from early September.
The dispute has led junior doctors to participate in a total of six strikes this year, including for the first time in the country’s national health service’s history.
Dr. Johann Malawana, a junior physician leader at the British Medical Association, resigned after a vote was boycotted on the contractual terms recommended by the British Medical Association.
In a meeting before 54,000 junior doctors and medical students voted, he told members of the British Medical Association that the deal was a good deal and should therefore be accepted.
After the vote, Health Minister Jeremy Hunt said that the contract would be enforced among health care workers in England.
Russia and Turkey are “out of alliance”?
A gesture ends a crisis.
The leaders of Russia and Turkey met on Tuesday as the two sides shook hands and officially ended the eight-month-long war of words and economic sanctions.
However, when Vladimir Putin greeted the Turkish president in the gilded lobby of a St. Petersburg palace, I had a very strong feeling that this reconciliation was what Ankara would most like to look forward to.
Yes, the two really shook hands.
However, even according to Mr. Putin's constant standards, Putin's simple smile, almost did not express enthusiasm.
Recep Tayyip Erdoğğan reportedly referred to “good friends” Putin five times.
“I can’t remember.
At the same time, he promised that relations with Russia will return to pre-crisis levels, or even higher.
The next day, a newspaper wrote that Erdoğan had behaved as if nothing had happened.
However, I think his enthusiasm hinted at something to the contrary.
However, Mr. Putin’s continued lukewarm attitude showed that he had forgotten nothing.
In fact, one of the first things he mentioned in his opening statement was the cause of the crisis: Turkey shot down a Russian warplane on the Syrian border.
The reaction in Moscow was fierce.
Mr. Putin lashed out at the incident, accusing Ankara of plotting Moscow behind it.
Attacks by so-called friends are even more unacceptable.
It’s hard, or even impossible, for both sides to re-establish real trust.
Since the beginning of November, Russian public opinion has also been reversed.
For several months, government-controlled media have carried out an astounding all-out attack on Ankara.
All of a sudden, the Turks seemed to take responsibility for everything.
Among them, the most serious is accusations from high-level officials that Erdogan’s family has profited from an illegal oil deal in the so-called Islamic State-controlled region of Syria.
He categorically denied the claim.
However, the news from St. Petersburg shows that it’s time to look ahead.
After all, the premise of this meeting was that Putin received an apology from Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan.
Russia can declare victory.
The benefits of a truce are clear for Ankara.
First of all, Erdoğan was nearly ousted after last month’s failed coup d’état, so he needs the support of all his friends.
Ongoing terrorist attacks have obviously hit him hard.
At the same time, there are economic motives.
Russia’s sanctions have dealt a big blow to Turkey, in particular the ban on charter flights that can transport millions of Russian tourists to Turkish shores every year.
This figure has fallen by almost 90%.
With the resumption of flights, Russian travel agencies and charter companies will surely breathe a sigh of relief.
At the end-of-the-season tour of the Mediterranean.
State television even predicted this week that the price of fruits and vegetables would fall once Turkey’s agricultural imports were re-opened.
A report in the Moscow newspaper Vedemosti said: "Visitors have gone, tomatoes are back."
The visit is also of additional political value for Moscow.
Ankara is outraged by the Western world’s weak reaction to takeover intentions.
In addition, Ankara has long been dissatisfied with the slow progress of EU accession negotiations and Putin's meddling, who is keen to take advantage of the political chill and weaken Turkey's relations with the Western world.
By championing the elected authorities in the aftermath of the failed coup d’état, the Russian leader is bound to receive an additional score from Ankara.
Note that this will be a gift for Moscow, which has always had a deep-seated fear of regime change.
As a result, a meeting held in this gilded seaside palace allowed Russia and Turkey to become an alliance between two countries that were rejected by the Western world and mistreated countries, which one analyst described as “unmatched.”
However, despite the public reconciliation, the two sides still have major differences.
Syria is one of the key factors. In recent days, Moscow has played a partisan role in Syria, while Russia and Turkey support opposing factions.
It can be predicted that after nearly three hours of preliminary talks, the two presidents said at the press conference that the topic had not yet been discussed.
Turkey's president shied away from the issue of differences between the two sides, while Putin emphasized it.
The two sides did not reach a clear consensus on how to seek common ground on the Syrian issue.
It would certainly be a good thing for the two leaders to resume dialogue once again amid months of open hostilities and a major disaster brought about by the downing of Russian warplanes by North Atlantic Treaty Organization member Turkey.
The Royal Bank of Scotland will no longer serve customers outside Scotland.
According to the chief executive of the Royal Bank of Scotland, the bank will be reduced to a logistics unit.
The Royal Bank of Scotland will no longer serve its customers outside of Scotland.
Ross McEwan told the BBC’s Scotland division that the Royal Bank of Scotland brand used to be committed to its global ambitions.
However, eight years ago on the verge of bankruptcy, he had to accept the bailout from then on and gave up that pursuit.
During that time, brand strategists used ‘RBS’ to protect other consumer finance brands.
The group has spent millions of pounds to sponsor international sporting events such as rugby Six Nations and Wimbledon tennis champion Andy Murray.
But for now, the right thing to do is to bring more national brands to prominence.
The Royal Bank of Scotland will be used by Scottish clients, but will not return to its original condition.
In England and Wales, all references outside the headquarters and stock exchanges will be changed to the National Westminster Bank.
The Ulster banking brand has been used by customers in the Republic of Ireland and the Autonomous Community of Northern Ireland.
