Russian President Vladimir Putin dismisses Sergey Ivanov's Chief of Staff
Russia’s President Vladimir Putin has unexpectedly dismissed Sergei Ivanov’s chief of staff.
For many years, Mr. Ivanov has been a part of a core group trusted by Mr. Putin.
The 63-year-old Ivanov has now been appointed as a special representative for environment and transport.
A Kremlin statement said Mr. Putin had “announced the removal of Ivanov’s chief of staff for the Russian president,” without giving a reason.
Anton Vaino, who has served as Mr. Ivanov’s deputy since 2012, has been appointed as his successor.
Vaino, 44, is a former diplomat.
Born in the Estonian capital Tallinn in 1972, he graduated from the prestigious Moscow State Institute of International Relations (MGIMO), where he worked at the Tokyo embassy.
Later, he was in charge of presidential proposals and government employees, the Kremlin website (in Russian).
After his appointment, he told Mr. Putin: “Thank you for your confidence.”
In my view, my Government's most important task is to support the heads of State in drafting laws and controlling the implementation.
Mr. Putin told Russian television on Friday that Mr. Ivanov himself had asked to resign and had recommended Mr. Vayno to take over.
Is such a smile convincing?
Moscow was puzzled by the move.
Sergey Ivanov has long been one of Putin’s closest aides and, like Putin, has worked for the Soviet Security Service (KGB).
As chief of staff, he was one of the most influential people in the country.
At a meeting broadcast on state television with President Vladimir Putin, both said the chief of staff had called for his resignation.
Despite a smile in front of the camera, almost no one was convinced – especially at this time before the parliamentary elections.
So, is this the result of a defeat in some kind of power struggle?
It has not yet been known.
However, the official statement that a once-bi presidential candidate suddenly chose to run Russia’s environmental policy – has aroused widespread skepticism.
The Kremlin's website quoted Mr. Ivanov's dialogue with Mr. Putin as saying: "Yes, at the beginning of 2012, in a dialogue, I asked you to give me a difficult position, or even a difficult position, for a four-year term."
"As a result, I've been the president's chief of staff for four or eight months."
Mr. Ivanov took up the post in December 2011.
Before that, he served as deputy prime minister and defense minister.
He’s a member of Russia’s Security Council and, like Mr. Putin, is a former member of the National Security Agency.
In the late 1990s, when Mr. Putin became director of the Federal Security Service (FSB, formerly the National Security Service), Mr. Ivanov was appointed as his deputy.
When Mr. Putin came to power, he also designated Mr. Ivanov as one of the five most trusted people.
It was once thought that Mr. Ivanov could become president of Russia at the end of Mr. Putin’s second term, because his re-election was unconstitutional.
However, the position ultimately belonged to Dmitry Medvedev, Putin’s other close aide.
Before returning to office as president after three-and-a-and-a-half, Mr. Putin served as Russia’s prime minister.
