A £2-a-day drug may reduce the threat of heart attack
Officials have suggested a £2-per-a-day drug would be rolled out to a larger number of patients, after which thousands of people with heart disease would benefit.
Anti-coagulant ticagrelor can reduce the risk of heart disease recurrence in people with heart disease.
Taking the drug for up to 12 months after a heart attack can reduce the risk of a stroke or recurrence of a heart attack.
National Institutes of Hygiene and Clinical Optimization (NICE), a regulator under the National Health Service (NHS), advises patients to take their medication continuously for four years in order to further reduce the risk of cardiovascular vascular disease.
But right now, NICE, the NHS watchdog, recommends that patients be given medication continuously for four years in order to further reduce the risk of cardiovascular vascular disease.
There are about 140,000 people suffering from heart disease every year in England, and a quarter of them will continue to experience a recurrence of a heart attack or a stroke.
Heart attacks and strokes are the result of fatty substances deposited in arterial walls to form plaques.
If the plaque is disconnected, it will lead to blood clots, which in turn will hinder the flow of blood into the heart and lead to a heart attack.
If the thrombosis moves, it will enter the bloodstream, hindering the flow of blood into the brain, resulting in a stroke.
Patients who have already experienced a heart attack have a higher risk of having another heart attack.
Tecagre, manufactured by the British company AstraZeneca (AstraZeneca) and sold under the trade name Brilique, can reduce the risk of disease by reducing the formation of blood thrombosis by reducing thrombosis.
The NICE draft guidance, released today, advises patients to take 90 mg of tikagret for 12 months, followed by 60 mg of tikagrele and twice a day for the next three years.
Professor Carole Longson, director of the NICE Center for Hygiene Technology Assessment, said: “Despite effective secondary prophylaxis, as many as a quarter of patients who have a history of heart attacks have re-emorrification or stroke, often with serious consequences.”
The fear of recurrence can have a large negative impact on a person’s quality of life.
The evidence has shown that tikagran with aspirin can effectively reduce the risk of heart disease recurrence and stroke in patients who have experienced a heart attack.
We are very pleased that the temporary recommendation of Tikagrei will provide additional treatment options for the tens of thousands of people who have benefited.
The draft guidance does not recommend the continued use of treatment beyond this period because of limited information on the efficacy and safety of ticagret after three years, in particular the risk of hemorrhage.
Computer culture makes ‘electropriation’ prevalent in Muslim regions
Lutfur Rahman, the former mayor of Tower Hamlets, was sacked last year for corruption, prompting the release of a government report.
“Political correctness” culture has turned a blind eye to widespread electoral fraud in the Muslim community, as this deadly government report reveals today.
Sir Eric Pickles, a former cabinet minister, accused the police, electoral watchdogs and town halls of turning a blind eye to evidence of the misuse of elections because of “oversensitivity to ethnic zoning and religious issues”.
The former minister of community affairs and the current anti-corruption chief said that the use of religious positions to intimidate the influence of voters, the police should be allowed to set up a cordon outside the polling station to protect panicked voters.
He advised voters to bring identity documents when they cast their ballots in order to verify their identity.
At present, voters only need to submit their names and addresses.
The report even recommended that voters' fingers can't be coated with wiping ink to prevent people from voting a second time.
Sir Eric said there should be greater protection for whistleblowers.
He called for an increase in the access of the media to parliamentary documentation and of decisions taken by unofficial committees or working groups.
“It’s not just about guarding against electoral fraud, it’s to protect local governments from a widespread culture of corruption and the financial fraud associated with them,” he said.
The report comes after the Tower Hamlets election scandal in East London was requested by Downing Street.
Last year, a special electoral court ruled that a series of corruption and violations committed by the current mayoral Rahman should be removed from office.
The court found that Rahman was “very ridiculously abusing” the religious sentiments of the Muslim community and remained silent on the charges of racial discrimination and Islamophobia.
Rachman is said to have “arclaimed during his election campaign that it is a religious duty to be loyal to Muslims”.
As many as 300 ballots are in jeopardy, which may have been cast on behalf of others.
Sir Eric said there was evidence of electoral fraud throughout the country.
In particular, he cautions against mail-in electoral fraud, and senior members of the Muslim community are said to have put pressure on voters to cast ballots for their designated candidates.
Sir Eric (in the photo) gave a number of suggestions for the clean-up voting mechanism, which included bringing a good identity card to prove his identity.
“The evidence shows that senior members, in particular those from Pakistani and Bangladeshi backgrounds, have put pressure on vulnerable groups in minority communities, particularly women and young people, to vote according to their wishes,” he said in the report.
“Because they are overly sensitive to racial division and religious ‘political correctness,’ they are concerned that State institutions will turn a blind eye to such acts.”
Sir Eric made 50 proposals to clean up the election mechanism, including a ban on political activists from handling mail-in voting documents to stop “voting gains”.
The report also calls for a strict examination of registration information to prevent voter rolls from being used for immigration and welfare fraud. Theresa May is also considering the report.
The recommendations included requiring voters to show their identity documents prior to voting.
After the vote, unobtainable ink on the voter's fingers to prevent people from voting for a second time.
Systematically verifies the nationality of the voters in order to ensure their eligibility to vote.
The validity period for automatic postal voting requests will be set at three years.
Prohibit political activists and activists from dealing with completed mail-and-mail voting envelopes.
The police are authorized to set up cordons around the polling stations.
Amend the provisions of the law to make it easier to sue those involved for intimidation in accordance with the Representation of the People Act 1983.
It is illegal to take a picture at a polling station.
It is stipulated that English can only be used at the polling stations (except Wales).
Strengthen the training to ensure that staff strictly comply with the “voters go to the polling room alone”.
Sir Eric lambasted the Metropolitan Police as "surprising" that they had not filed any criminal charges following the Tower Hamlets incident.
"Rahman and his electoral officers have a lot of corruption and violations, so they have been disqualified by the electoral court," he said.
According to the standard of criminal proof, he is unmistakably culpable in many ways.
He has also criticized the "tick box inspection" that the election registration department of the town hall is false.
Sir Eric said: “Last year’s court was a wake-up call to Tower Hamlets.”
Our country has a glorious tradition of “the mother of parliament”, but the private and covert electoral fraud of certain institutions and the subsequent perpetuation are really worrying, which also threatens this good reputation.
