Reid: If Hillary is elected president, she will nominate Garland to the Supreme Court.
Senate Democratic leader Harry Reid has said he is certain she will nominate Judge Merrick Garland to the Supreme Court if Democratic presidential candidate Hillary Clinton is elected president.
Senate Republicans have blocked confirmation of Garland since President Barack Obama nominated Garland in March.
Senate Majority Leader Mitch McConnell has said the next president will replace the late Supreme Court Justice Antonin Scalia.
Reid said in a teleconference on Thursday that he predicted that Clinton would choose Garland with “some credibility.”
A spokeswoman for Reid later said that Reid did not talk directly with Clinton about whether he would nominate Garland, adding that he was just speculating.
He praised Garland and said Clinton’s team didn’t want to “disrupt” with the new candidates.
Garland is the chief judge of the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit for the District of Columbia.
Reid said that the Republicans who blocked Garland’s nomination were the “favorites” and “achievers” of Republican presidential candidate Donald Trump.
He said Trump was unfit to be president.
