French court dismisses petition to demolish shops in jungle refugee camp
A court in France has rejected the Calais government’s intention to demolish dozens of makeshift shops and restaurants in the “jungle” refugee camp, which is currently home to thousands of refugees.
At this week's court's hearing, the Calais government argued that the French government should have the power to demolish a total of 72 makeshift shops in the camp. People sell goods ranging from cigarettes to energy drinks, provide haircut services, and also sell tea for 50 cents (40 pence) in makeshift restaurants or sell basic meals for a couple of euros.
The office of the governor of Calais believes that these places should be demolished, because they are a parallel economy that does not pay taxes, and there are still fires and health hazards.
Humanitarian groups told the court that the shops and restaurants were extremely important, as the free food provided by state-supported associations and other groups simply did not meet the growing needs of the refugees in the camp.
These makeshift shops and restaurants often provide shelter and free food for those in need, they said.
A Lille court ruled that there was no legal basis to evict the operators of the 72 temporary stores, but said the governor’s concerns were “entirely understandable”.
Judge Jean François Molla stated that the role of these grocery stores, cafes and restaurants goes far beyond simply providing food to refugees who “live in extremely dangerous conditions”.
He said that these places are a peaceful meeting point between refugees and volunteers.
One of the places under threat of demolition is the camp’s “Jungle Book Kids,” a non-profit shop that offers 200 daily food, English and French lessons, as well as asylum advice for hundreds of unaccompanied and vulnerable minors in the camp.
More than 170,000 people signed a petition to protect the cafe.
In a letter addressed to the Guardian, it said the closure of the cafe “would be a disaster for these children (many of whom are only eight years old).”
Help Refugees and L'Auberge des Migrants, two charitable organisations, predicted on Friday that the number of refugees in the bush camps had exceeded 9,000, the highest level since the surge in the number of refugees in the summer.
