The British team won the silver medal in the rugby sevens, and the Fiji team won the gold medal.
In Thursday night’s rugby final, the British team failed to extend their pre-emerged performance in front of Fiji, which was beaten 43-7 by Fiji at the Tiodoro Stadium.
The Fiji team won the gold medal with an impeccable performance in terms of possession, steal, avoidance, cooperation, running and strength, which is also the country's first-ever Olympic medal.
It's also a no small consolation for the defeated British team, which the British delegation won the third silver medal of the day. It's a result that's enough to make the English rugby team proud: they beat New Zealand, Argentina, and South Africa all the way to the sevens rugby for their first time to the Olympic Games.
Earlier in the day, Britain narrowly defeated South Africa 7-5, which may have affected their status in the final.
In any case, the current Fiji team is strong enough to beat a full-fledged 15-man team.
Tomorrow, at least one Briton will wear a gold medal, which may have been a consolation, but it may not have been possible for the English team. This Englishman is Fiji head coach from Wimbledon Ben Ryan, who has served as the head coach of the British team.
It will not be easy for the British team to achieve today's results.
Eight years ago, under Ryan's management, they did not win the Adélaid international sevens rugby.
Now they’re just one step away from the championship.
Although they have taken a huge hit, they have also made amazing progress.
Fiji's opening had an advantage, leaving the British team with no effort.
For Tom Mitchell and his teammates, it's like bumping into a series of moving buses. By the time Ossie Colliniso stormed the horned-flag zone, the World Series championship team was already 5-0 far ahead.
Just four minutes into the game, Jerry Tuhuai scored again on the right-wing touchdown.
At 12-0, the British team barely touched the ball.
Mitchell's forceful interdiction left the giant-ordinary Leon Nakalava without a third-scoring, but it was only delaying Fiji's score.
At the seven-minute, Yasa Wailemaru was in the right-hand corner. At 17–0, the British team made it even worse.
Karawa scored another point, and there was only one minute left in the first half of the game.
Watmo Lavowo then broke through Britain’s defense and went straight to the scoring line.
At half-time, the score was already 29-0.
It’s as if the Fijians were born to play rug rugby, and the British team is still new.
That's the way it is.
England made a huge change in the second half and pounded the Fijians' defence for a full four minutes, which had been good in terms of the first half. But then the Fiji team made a brilliant match, with Joshua Tuesova scoring again.
Dan Norton, who has created a scoring opportunity for the United Kingdom, has changed the score to 36 to 7, but the game has long been under the control of the South China Sea island, and their fans are on the platform singing, waving flags and singing and dancing.
The excellent performance of the Fiji team made it necessary to clapping their hands.
Marta Willem blocked an unrelenting attack from Fiji at the last minute.
The final whistle sounded and the Fijians hugged each other, and the defeated British team could only regret the withdrawal.
Earlier in the same day, Fiji defeated Japan 20-5 in the semi-final, while Britain narrowly defeated South Africa in an intense semi-final showdown.
In the first half, Kyle Brown helped South Africa take the lead, but Dan Norton scored in the second half with an excellent footwork.
After that, England's Anthony's brother Marcus Watson ensured the team's victory with a wonderful interception, and the British team finally won the silver medal.
Helen Graff and Heather Stanning, inspired by cancer-damaged coaches, won a rowing gold medal.
It’s no exaggeration to say that it’s one of the most exciting gold medals.
From the first paddle, Helen Graff and Heather Steining have received 220 punitive lifting trainings, and the gold medal they have won in Estacio da Lagoa has even more extraordinary significance.
Although there were only two of the paddles, there was another person on the boat – coach Robin Williams – whose voice accompanies them for the second consecutive women’s double paddle Olympic gold medal, the UK’s first rowing women’s group to reach this height.
They have an undefeated record of 39 games and have an absolute advantage.
“What we’ve seen is these two talented athletes moving gracefully on the water, but that’s not the whole point of this victory.
There are a lot of people who volunteer to help behind every rowing gold, and it’s their continuous dedication that makes a difference, and the biggest help is a coach who learned 30 months ago that he has cancer and doesn’t know if he’ll be able to live to see the day.
As they say, death can come at any moment.
I had bladder cancer and they had surgery for me.
Without surgery, cancer cells will spread throughout the body.
In this way, the situation will be much worse.
This will slightly change your view.
Williams was diagnosed with cancer in December 2013.
Fortunately, he started treatment in early 2014 that minimized the impact, although it might not be appropriate to say so in such a situation.
I need to get paid, so I have to get better quickly.
It’s an motivator for me.
I’m in the hospital, and even though they say a few steps is enough, I still walk around the ward at a distance of 20 feet, 30, 50 feet.
That’s the starting point for everything.
Boat racing itself is a journey, and it makes the journey a little longer.
The first thing that came to their mind at the moment of winning the gold was Williams. This morning's Williams was a little bit talkier than usual, in order to overcome his anxieties.
Stanning, a former Royal Artillery team chief for Afghanistan, said: "I'm in a bad mood this week, it's probably a little extreme, it's a bit extreme, but this gold medal does make a lot of sense."
“It repeatedly tells me how important it is to me and how hard it is for me, Helen and Robin.
“It’s a three-year reward for Helen, Robin, and me.
“If there’s no Robin, we’re just nameless, thank you very much, he’s the best coach in the world.”
“I can’t possibly find a better trainer and coach than him.
Helen Graff and Heather Steining did not lose in five years.
The penalties imposed by this combination are incomparable to their opponents.
Even in the last 500 meters, when New Zealand and Denmark began to approach, Stanning and Graff still have a lot of physical reserves.
At that time it was thrilling.
Helen said, “Keep the pace, keep the tempo.”
It’s very important not to be influenced by an opponent.
We’ve had a good start, and at the same time we’re not looking forward to doing something earth-shattering.
“We didn’t think about today’s glory, we just wanted to tie every paddle and win this game, and we did.
It took a year for the group to determine its own future.
Graff will be married next month by TV adventurer Steve Bakshall.
But for now, she just wants to enjoy the moment.
“I don’t want to say it’s my last game.
This will put too much pressure on me.
It took me about six months in London to realize I’m an Olympic champion.
It’s a whole new experience, and I’m very nervous.
It’s been about 4 minutes now.
She said, "I'm feeling better."
Unbelievable structure of the Los Angeles Central Library
The Southern California chapter of the American Academy of Architectural Historians held a salon with Arnold Swartzmann and Steven Ge, the authors of the new Los Angeles Central Museum: A History of Art and Architecture. Viewers can learn more about the history of the central library downtown through the event.
Ji and Swartzmann will discuss and share the design of Betren Gudsey in 1926 and the restoration and expansion of Hadi Holzman Vervl’s office in 1933.
The Salon will take place on Aug. 21 from 2 p.m. to 4 p.m. in Salle Moderne, Art Deco Southwestern School of Law (formerly Bullocks Wilshire), 3050 Wilshire Blvd.
Participants will be able to visit the central hall, the era exhibition room, the cactus lounge and the tea room.
Advance booking is required; SAHSCC members: $15; non-members: $25.
