Inflation in Bulgaria for first time in eight months
According to the national statistics, the monthly inflation rate in Bulgaria in July was 1%.
This is the highest rate of inflation in several years (since June 2012). For the first time since October last year (inflation rate of 0.2%), the inflation rate exceeded zero.
Compared to the previous 12 months (August 2014 to July 2015), the average annual inflation rate for the past 12 months (August 2015 to July 2016) was -0.8% (measured by the consumer price index).
Compared with June 2016, the consumer price index in July 2016 was 101.0%, which means that the monthly inflation rate was 1.0%.
The inflation rate so far this year (July 2016 compared to December 2015) is -0.2%. Compared to July 2015, the annual inflation rate in July 2016 is -0.2%.
Compared with the previous month, the price of the following categories of consumer goods increased in July 2016: food and non-alcoholic beverages (1.7%), transportation (1.8%), entertainment and culture (6.3%), restaurants and hotels (1.2%), and other goods and services (0.3%).
Clothing and footwear (2.2%), furniture, home appliances and conventional maintenance (0.5%), and medical (0.1%).
Alcoholic beverages and tobacco, housing, water, electricity, natural gas and other fuels, telecommunications and education remained at the same price as last month.
Snapchat’s ‘anime’ effect accused of satirizing Asians
Snapchat said the filter was inspired by anime, but some observers consider it to be racial.
Snapchat’s 150 million everyday users can change the reality with its filtering tools and turn portraits into hilarity.
You can transform yourself into a pineapple, a dog, or a Roy Lichtenstein character.
These lenses have the effect of passivation and bending characteristics, resulting in more than 30 million modified selfies every day.
Any mistake will be immediately noticed by the public.
Snapchat lenses have been criticized, and the app has been accused of agitating the role of a black person or the beauty of white skin.
Therefore, when the app this week provides some users with a filter that can create oblique eyes, teeth, and pink face effects, some commentators have called it a racial satire of “yellow people” in Asia.
They even think that these recurring problems mean that the company has big problems in terms of diversity.
The Verge and Motherboard report sparked outrage on Wednesday after Snapchat said it removed the feature.
The company's explanation is that the filter pays homage to anime characters, rather than satirizing Asians.
But for observers who have lived through racism, the filter reminds them of a harmful stereotype in reality.
Others are also staunchly opposed to these anime images.
In an email, a Korean-American art student who tweeted about the photos and was widely shared said the filters were “at least distressing and uncomfortable.”
She added: “It is difficult to distinguish between extremely disgusting images of Asian satire and the effects of filters.”
Although it’s not just the yellow people in the filter, the yellow people are extremely devalued.
Snapchat isn’t the only company that’s scratching the bottom line.
From the company’s board of trustees to Hollywood and the devices we carry, American culture seems to have always promoted diversity and inclusion.
Snapchat’s large young population is likely to be very sensitive because it pays more attention to racial diversity than the older generation.
When one of Snapchat’s filters created an image that insulted users, the 25-year-old said in an interview on Thursday: “It’s even harder to get out of sight than it used to.”
On Thursday, Ms. Zhu, a product manager and engineer at Medium, decided to delete her Snapchat account and encourage others to do the same.
In an article written for Medium and in a telephone interview, she stated that she believes the racial controversies reflect Snapchat’s lack of racial diversity in the hiring process.
Ms. Zhu criticized the company’s employees for being mostly white, with leadership entirely male and putting a hashtag at the end of the article: #DeleteSnapchat.
As a Chinese-American, Ms. Zhu said, “It’s either that they don’t have a variety of people in their workforce to make such a decision, or there are some people of color among their employees, but their position doesn’t allow them to speak freely.”
Other observers shared a complaint in which she had not been responded to by Snapchat.
The company doesn’t release data on the diversity of its employees and says it’s a private company.
On Thursday, Snapchat declined to discuss the ethnic backgrounds of its employees, but a spokesperson said the company recently hired a recruiter who specializes in paying attention to a lower percentage of people inside and promoting in-house inclusion.
Ms. Zhu said that she would close her Snapchat account: "I want to know if they need more users like us who are opposed to it."
Congressional panel says military officials distort ISIS intelligence
In a report released on Thursday, a congressional task force noted that U.S. Central Command officials changed the intelligence report to describe the war against the Islamic State in Iraq and Syria more optimistic than it actually is.
The interim report comes from a special task force formed by the Republican chairman of the House Armed Services Committee, the Intelligence Committee, and the House Appropriations Committee's defense sub-committee. The report said there was "widespread dissatisfaction" among Central Command's intelligence analysts, who said their superiors were falsifying their assessment of the U.S. war with the Islamic State.
Central Command, based in Tampa, Florida (Centcom) oversees U.S. military operations in the Middle East and Central Asia.
According to a piece of information on the report, “Intelligence vetted by Central Command’s senior leaders is more optimistic about U.S. counter-terrorism operations than it really is, and has always been more optimistic than the analysis of other intelligence agencies.”
A representative of the Republican Party in California said: "Central Command's behavior is unacceptable. On the one hand, our fighters are suffering from war, and on the other is that top policymakers receive the wrong analysis."
Central Command’s leadership issues have been extended to the highest levels of the agency.
The report details Central Command’s long-standing problems in describing and analyzing U.S. training of Iraqi forces in 2014 and 2015.
“While there is no conclusive evidence that senior Obama administration officials ordered changes to these reports, according to the report’s descriptions, the analysts felt pressured by CENTCOM leaders to give a more optimistic view of the threat posed by the Islamic State (ISIS or ISIL).
“In the first half of 2015, many Central Command press releases, statements and congressional testimony were far more optimistic than they actually were,” the report said.
For example, a Central Command official has publicly said it could take a large-scale military operation as early as April or May 2015 to retake Mosul.
Mosul, Iraq’s second-largest city, is still under Islamic State control.
Mike Pompeo, the Republican representative for Kansas, said in a statement: "After months of investigation, this result has become very clear."
From mid-2014 to mid-2015, much of U.S. Central Command’s high-level intelligence officials controlled the command’s intelligence products, concealing the Islamic State’s threat in Iraq.
After the Republican Party learned from analysts that intelligence about the Islamic State had been manipulated, it set up a special working group.
As the investigation progresses, there will be many more to be found after the report, which was released on Thursday.
The Attorney General of the Ministry of Defense has also conducted a long-term investigation of the Central Command.
Democratic members of the House Intelligence Committee released their findings on Thursday, in line with some of the conclusions of the GOP task force.
“Between 2014 and 2015, Central Command’s intelligence assessments of Islamic State and Iraqi peacekeeping forces developed an overly Narrow process.”
Mr Sheanda stated that the process “implied the release of intelligence”, lowered the morale of the analysts, and “cannot accept different opinions”.
But Mr Sheanda and the Democrats also said that they found no evidence that the White House was trying to put pressure on Central Command’s analysts at a time when their conclusions were in line with a “prejudgment or a political narrative.”
The Daily Beast reported the preliminary results of a congressional investigation on Tuesday.
In August last year, the New York Times reported that the Pentagon’s attorney general began an investigation after receiving a complaint from a Central Command analyst.
In a statement, Pentagon spokesman Navy Maj. Patrick Evans said in a statement that the Defense Department does not make any comment on this congressional report before the attorney general’s investigation ends.
“Experts are sometimes at odds over the interpretation of complex data, and intelligence agencies and the Department of Defense encourage a healthy dialogue on these important national security topics,” he said.
The impact of the investigation has outweighed the success of U.S.-led bombing operations in Iraq and in Syria, claimed by a growing number of Defense Department officials.
But over the past year, the claims have led the public to question the extent to which President Barack Obama can trust the assessments of military operations in Iraq, Afghanistan, Libya, and other crisis-producing areas issued by Central Command.
Criticism of Islamic State intelligence is reminiscent more than a decade of controversies, when Central Command’s intelligence-analyst Gregory Hooke wrote a research piece in which George W. Bush’s administration insisted on sending small-scale troops to Iraq despite the objections of numerous analysts and making little consideration of what happened after the 2003 invasion.
Mr. Hooke played a key role in this campaign against Islamic State’s intelligence.
Last year, officials said Mr. Hook’s team’s conclusion that airstrikes against the Islamic State’s oil refinery did not significantly reduce the terrorist group’s economic revenues, as it built a temporary refinery to continue to sell oil on the black market, contrary to the announcement.
According to a report by Time magazine in September, the Central Command did not disclose the discovery.
After multiple lawyers reviewed Senate testimony about an over-optimistic assessment of the war, Central Command commander Lloyd Austen III was criticized last year.
General Austin was decommissioned in April and is currently replaced by Joseph Votel from his position at Central Command.
The U.S. won four in a row in the women's hockey game, which will go to war with the United Kingdom.
Katie Bam, who won the 6-1 victory over Japan, took the lead twice for the U.S. women’s hockey team on Thursday to help the U.S. beat India 3-0.
Five goals in two games is a thrilling four, but the 27-year-old says she doesn’t know she’s performing so well.
“I don’t know anything about that.”
“I’m not the kind of person who remembers a few goals.
All she knows is that the U.S. team, which has won four in a row at the Olympic Games, will compete for the first in the group with Britain, which has not suffered a 2-0 victory over Japan on Thursday.
The United Kingdom and the United States met narrowly in the final match of the first round on Saturday.
The two teams advance to the quarter-finals, which will determine who will appear as the first in the group.
The team that finished first in the group would avoid encountering a strong Dutch team, which would be able to go even further.
The U.S. team performed a miracle with an astounding performance on Saturday. Be aware that at the London Olympics four years ago, the U.S. team was at the bottom of 12.
Bam was also in that team at the time.
Bam grew up in Brubel, Pennsylvania, first picked up a hockey stick at the age of 3 (her twin sister played the sport in Drexel) and joined the national team at the age of 16, becoming the youngest member of the national team to date.
Failing to reach the final list, she missed the 2008 Olympics.
In 2012, she and her teammates were not well-received.
The following year, Englishman Craig Panham served as head coach and everything began to change.
“This is a change in culture, mentality and substance.”
We had not yet reached our current level in London.
We came with a complete turnaround.
On Thursday night, Bam took the lead in the first half with a close-range back-and-for-the-hand shot.
India has not yet tasted victory in this competition, but this team has shown an amazing tenacity and maintained a 1-0 score until the end of the third quarter, when Bam broke out.
She carried the ball along the left side of the court, and then cut into the middle and pumped into the net, the United States led 2-0.
Melisa Gonzales scored the final goal in the middle of the fourth quarter.
U.S. goalkeeper Jackie Bridges scored a goal for the first time in the tournament, scoring a goal in the first three victories.
This time she played very normally.
Bam as well.
Panam said: "We're in a good state right now, but exactly how good it will be depends on the outcome of Saturday's game.
Shabby food from the Great Depression.
In March 1933, Franklin Delano Roosevelt, who had just been elected president, was sitting in the president’s office for lunch.
As a gourist, President Roosevelt loved Fifth Avenue delicacies such as foie gras and Maryland stewed waterfish.
On that day, his menu was very simple: ketchup devil eggs, mashed potatoes and pudding as a snack.
Jane Ziegman said: "This is to share the pain with the people who are suffering in food."
Her husband, Andrew Ko, added: “It also reflects the American way of eating.”
The couple, who lived in Brooklyn Heights, are food historians.
Mr. Coe’s previous book, “Frying Cigarettes,” was about Chinese food in the United States, and Ms. Ziegman told the lives of slum tenants through food in her 97 Orchard.
Their co-contributory book, “Abundant Meals,” will be published on Tuesday by Harper, about America’s food history during the Great Depression.
After showing some common insights on food, they met a reporter at Aisenberg’s sandwich shop. This simple lunch counter in the iron district has been open since 1929 when the economy collapsed.
The 54-year-old Ms. Ziegman ordered a creamy cheese with a crushed olive sandwich, while the 57-year-old ordered a platter of turkeys, mashed potatoes, and vegetable platters.
The reporter clicked on a baking roll, and the couple thought that the dishes were suitable to discuss the Depression-era diet.
Ms. Ziegman said, “Bread was very popular at that time.”
At that time, there was peanut bread, liver bread, and soy bread.
They are all made with a raw material and a cheap dome that can be poured on the raw material.
Imagine that your dinners are all peanuts.
Mr. Ke played a strange appearance.
He said, "It's going to be like lead in your stomach."
In the years leading up to the Great Depression, U.S. Americans were a buffet, especially in the suburbs.
A group of women brewed meals for male farm workers with just-examined pies for breakfast, lunch, and dinner.
During World War I, it was the Americans who gave their food to hungry Europeans. Abundant Meal chronicles how the country had suddenly ceased to be a land of prosperity.
Explaining why he and his wife were writing the book: “At that time, food had become a major source of displeasure among Americans.”
Another unpleasant topic is shabby new foods: “fascinated foods” such as bread and stew have become the main dishes, and stew is “the best way to cook leftovers,” Ms. Zigman said, because chefs can use the cream in the sauce to cover up the tasty ingredients.
Fresh fruits are replaced by cheap dried fruits.
The meat that has been the main character on the American table for many years has become a rudimentary luxury.
The strange smorgas used to only think about calorie, and the first thing people think about is nutrition compared to the taste and even the basic common sense of cooking.
In the course of researching the book, which included various recipes, Ms. Ziegman produced a baked onion filled with peanut oil.
“It’s not very common at the dinner table.”
Ms Ziegman said: “It’s too strange.”
Peanut oil and baked onions simply cannot be matched.
This is characteristic of many family meals.
Family economists influenced the country’s eating habits, such as Louis Stanley, who served as the head chef of the Federal Office for Family Economics from 1923 to 1943, which is unique in history.
They published recipes and articles in newspapers and magazines, encouraging women to “finish carefully” and dare to turn bad foods such as creamy pasta with carrots into delicacies.
It’s a “premnant food.”
It’s especially interesting about the government’s response to hungry people, such as President Herbert Hoover’s “I can’t control other people’s affairs.”
Although he publicly plauded the simple Iowa farmhouses of his childhood, his diet was as refined as a millionaire.
He said: "He likes to eat European Western food, such as cucumber sauce with cucumber sauce."
Wearing an evening dress, he dined in a gilded restaurant.
He was clearly separated from the people.
President Roosevelt would probably have made the same mistake if his wife Eleanor had encouraged him to set an example in his diet.
According to the book, Ms. Roosevelt's butler had no interest in the taste, so "the food in the White House is not only the hardest food in Washington, but also the worst food for cooking."
As soon as the United States came back to formality, many of the methods of cooking during the Great Depression were abandoned.
“What’s left is our focus on calorie and nutrition and the application of science to cooking.
In writing the book, the couple said they learned more about Ms Ziegman’s mother’s eating habits, such as how she didn’t throw away food.
“She’s very afraid of wasting food.”
Ms. Ziegman recalled: “She actually made a lot of people sick.”
For example, there has been a "cream puff" incident.
After removing the plates, she and Mr. Cor brought out a dessert they had prepared at home: this was the pudding that President Roosevelt ate for lunch.
“It’s called creamy fruit,” Ms. Zygman said, adding that a trendy name for an ordinary dish was “the usual trick.”
There is no cream in this dessert, only fruit, flour, sugar, water and cinnamon.
It’s hard for everyone to dig deep into the sticky brown paste.
Unsurprisingly, the dessert tastes good.
He ate a second spoon and a third spoon.
He felt that it was an affordable dessert, so he told his wife, "It's better to add broken walnuts."
Unfortunately, OPEC's latest monthly report shows that crude oil production continued to increase in July, and Saudi Arabia's output reached a new high.
This report does not prohibit the market from doubting the credibility of OPEC, and the road to oil price recovery is likely to still be full of thorns.
On Monday, OPEC’s rotating chairman and Qatar’s energy minister said OPEC would hold an informal meeting on the sidelines of an international energy forum on Sept. 26-28.
OPEC's monthly report released on Wednesday showed that the entire OPEC organization's production, including Gabon, rose by 46,000 barrels per day in July to 33.11 million barrels per day.
OPEC also said in the monthly report that low crude oil prices have prompted global refiners to produce more refined oil products, thereby increasing the degree of oversupply in the market.
Affected by this news, coupled with U.S. higher crude oil production expectations and an unexpected increase in crude oil inventories, international oil prices fell for two consecutive days.
As of Wednesday's close, light crude oil futures on the New York Mercantile Exchange were down 2.48 per cent in September, and London Brent crude futures for October delivery were down 2.07 per cent.
According to Reuters, the proposed oil-producing country meeting is unlikely to significantly tighten the supply, and the data released by the US Energy Information Agency also suppress market popularity.
Iran's official news agency, Fars on Wednesday, quoted Iran's oil minister as saying that Iran's daily crude oil production was as high as 3.85 million barrels, the highest level since 2008.
At the news of the meeting, Russian Energy Minister Alexander Novak said there is no need for a new round of frozen production negotiations under oil prices.
It’s already in April, when OPEC members failed to reach an agreement on any measures.
Emma Richards, an oil and gas analyst at BMI Research, said markets and the media were likely to overreact to OPEC’s upcoming meeting.
Helima Croft, head of commodities strategy at the Royal Bank of Canada, said OPEC was changing the mood by releasing a signal that they would broker a deal.
It’s like smoke and a mirror (deliberately making the wrong impression).
Eugen Weinberg, head of commodity research at Commerzbank, said there was a low likelihood that OPEC would try to stabilise the market through the September meeting.
This is likely to further undermine the market's confidence in OPEC, after all, the organization staged a "wolf".
All localities should use medical institutions that have posted medical advertisements, centralized complaints and reports, and have been punished many times in the past as the focus of spot checks.
The National Health and Family Planning Commission and the State Administration of Traditional Chinese Medicine will promptly organize the special supervision and inspection work carried out in some provinces.
According to media disclosures, due to the large-scale influx of capital into Internet medicine, the homogenization of the current products is serious, the investment boom is gradually receding, and capital has entered a wait-and-see period.
In addition, according to data from Hong Kong's Alipi Hospital Management and Research Center, a large number of Internet medical companies have died within a year, and about 20% of the start-up Internet medical companies have ceased to exist.
However, just as most Internet medical companies are struggling to raise funds in Series A or B, there are several leading companies in subdivided areas who can still gain enthusiasm for capital.
It seems that Internet medical entrepreneurship must not be "a swarm", but it is necessary to look at the needs of the market, sink into the heart, and do deep.
Yuyue invested 1200.00 million yuan, accounting for 40% of the registered capital, and Kyushutong Equipment Company invested 1800.00 million yuan, accounting for 60% of the registered capital.
In recent years, with the country's policy support for medical informatization, the electronic and integrated supply chain management system has gradually entered hospitals at all levels.
The cooperation with Kyushu Tong is precisely aimed at the needs of the market, helping the hospital to build an advanced supply chain management system, and taking this as an opportunity to seek more cooperation.
Different from many Internet medical companies, iBaby has been focusing on the obstetric vertical field of the Internet from the very beginning, plowing the two ends of pregnant mothers and doctors.
The pregnant mother can not only free 24-hour and one-to-one doctor consultation, but can also pay for targeted medical consultations.
On the doctor's side, iBaby broke the geographical restrictions in the form of online lectures, peer-to-peer exchanges, case discussions and other micro-leversaries organized by organizing experts to grass-roots doctors.
At present, there are three sections on the doctor's side, which are doctor's continuing education, doctor's socialization and doctor's help.
Provide services such as e-learning, case discussion, and academic exchanges; social platforms between doctors in the same hospitals and departments; legal aid and entrepreneurship support needed for doctor-patient relationships or personal lives.
According to media reports, CEO Wang Lei recently said that Ali's health is "willing to calm down" to win the big data of health.
Internet companies and data companies lack professionalism, and the writing method on the same term can not be unified.
Ali's current layout in the field of medical and health is divided into three parts: Ali Health, Tmall Medical Museum and Alipay Future Hospital.
Wang Lei admitted that medical and health entrepreneurship will be more difficult than other industries, "This is not just a matter of how much money, but also to calm down, to endure loneliness slowly to do."
Watching the Olympic events is easy to damage the heart.
The Olympic Games in Brazil are in full swing, and even in the middle of the night when the event is broadcast in the country, many citizens will stay up at night to watch.
In this regard, the doctor specially reminded that there are health risks in staying up late to watch the event, which may lead to cardiovascular disease.
If you stay up late for a long period of time, the hormone will still be a lot of secretion, the human body will continue to be in a state of tension and excitement, can not get a normal recovery, it is easy to cause vascular constriction and blood pressure rise, resulting in cardiovascular disease.
In particular, Han Zhiyuan reminded that people with cardiovascular risk factors such as high blood pressure, high blood sugar, high blood lipids, smoking habits, and family history will be more likely to lead to cardiovascular disease if they stay up late.
If you stay up late for a long time, not only the cardiovascular function will be affected, but the gastrointestinal function, the immune system, and the neuroendocrine system will be affected, and even the risk of tumors will increase.
For elderly friends, Han Zhiyuan suggested that try not to stay up late in order to watch the Olympic Games, especially for groups with a history of cardiovascular disease.
During the last "World Cup" football game, Han Zhiyuan received a patient in his more than 60-year-old, and his myocardial infarction was caused or related to staying up late to watch the game.
Since the beginning of this year, its department has treated a number of patients with myocardial infarctions or strokes around the age of about 25, which is largely caused by the patient's long-term lifestyle.
Do not stay up late every day, occasionally stay up late to watch the favorite sports, other projects may wish to wait for the next day to watch the replay of the event.
When staying up at night, add water and eat more fruits, which can prevent vascular congestion and enhance the body's immunity.
Beijing time on August 12th news, the 2016 Rio Olympic Games women's 50 meters rifle three posture final battle, Chinese veteran Du Li won the bronze medal.
Du Li has become the senior of the four Olympic dynasties, winning a total of 2 gold, 1 silver and 1 copper and four medals in the four Olympic Games.
In 1998, he entered the shooting team of Shandong Province and followed coach Wang Devin for professional shooting training.
In June 2003, the women's 10-meter air Rifle at the shooting World Cup in Croatia broke the world record with a 504.9 ring.
In August 2004, he won the gold medal of the women's 10m air rifle, winning the first gold medal for the Chinese team at the Olympic Games in Athens.
In August 2008, he won the gold medal with a total score of 690.3 in the 50-meter sports rifle.
In August 2012, after ousting from the London Olympic Games, Doo Li applied for temporary decommissioning to the International Shooting Federation.
In January 2015, Tu Li announced that she would return to the national team to start training and prepare for the Rio Olympics.
In March 2016, at the end of the Rio Olympic qualifiers, she once again qualified for the women's 10-meter air Rifle Olympics after eight years.
On August 6, 2016, in the women's ten-meter air rifle event at the Rio Olympic Games, Doo Li won the silver medal with a score of 207 rings.
On August 12, 2016, he won the bronze medal in the women's 50-meter Rifle three poses competition at the Rio Olympics.
The film is expected to premiere early next year on China Central Television and LeTV, and will also launch a VR panorama documentary version for the first time.
